00:30:29,720 --> 00:30:32,600
and led to bloodshed and violence in France.

490
00:30:32,600 --> 00:30:36,440
And as a critique, they were called this group

491
00:30:36,440 --> 00:30:39,680
of Republicans, the Democratic Republicans.

492
00:30:39,680 --> 00:30:42,320
It was meant to be a diss.

493
00:30:44,040 --> 00:30:47,040
Jefferson and his supporters decided to adopt this

494
00:30:47,040 --> 00:30:48,880
almost as a point of honor

495
00:30:48,880 --> 00:30:52,000
and called themselves the Democratic Republicans.

496
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:54,320
And this was the founding of what we know today

497
00:30:54,320 --> 00:30:56,040
as the Democratic Party.

498
00:30:56,040 --> 00:31:00,480
And how are their beliefs related to what we think of now and we think of Democrats?

499
00:31:00,480 --> 00:31:02,040
Okay, here's Kanisha Grant.

500
00:31:02,040 --> 00:31:05,200
She is a professor of political science at Howard University.

501
00:31:05,200 --> 00:31:09,740
So when we think about the Democratic Party at that time, we don't think of it anything

502
00:31:09,740 --> 00:31:11,800
like the Democratic Party at this time.

503
00:31:11,800 --> 00:31:17,080
The Democratic Party at that time is liberal with a lower case L, as scholars say.

504
00:31:17,080 --> 00:31:23,120
And that means that they don't want to see the government being very active.

505
00:31:23,120 --> 00:31:27,040
The government should not be involved in your life telling you what to do.

506
00:31:27,040 --> 00:31:31,880
The government should just kind of be around to make sure that things don't fall apart.

507
00:31:31,880 --> 00:31:34,680
Which is different from the party as we think about it today.

508
00:31:34,680 --> 00:31:38,640
We think about a Democratic party today as one who's willing to step in to try to correct

509
00:31:38,640 --> 00:31:45,240
some of the perceived wrongs they might say in the economy or some of the perceived wrongs

510
00:31:45,240 --> 00:31:48,840
in the way that we treat humans and these other kinds of things.

511
00:31:48,840 --> 00:31:51,080
How does it change?

512
00:31:51,080 --> 00:31:54,480
Because that to me is like 180 degrees.

513
00:31:54,480 --> 00:31:55,320
All right, we'll get there.

514
00:31:55,320 --> 00:31:57,480
And that is Kanisha's particular bailiwick.

515
00:31:57,480 --> 00:31:59,560
But first there is a big shift,

516
00:31:59,560 --> 00:32:02,760
and it starts with Andrew Jackson in 1829.

517
00:32:02,760 --> 00:32:06,040
By the time Andrew Jackson is president,

518
00:32:06,040 --> 00:32:10,520
he has dropped the Republican from his affiliation.

519
00:32:10,520 --> 00:32:14,520
So he identifies himself as a Democratic candidate.

520
00:32:14,520 --> 00:32:17,480
Andrew Jackson was the southerner.

521
00:32:17,480 --> 00:32:20,040
He was a slave owner.

522
00:32:20,040 --> 00:32:22,560
He was a war hero.

523
00:32:22,560 --> 00:32:25,720
He championed, even though he was a wealthy landowner,

524
00:32:25,720 --> 00:32:30,600
he championed the idea of sort of the ordinary man,

525
00:32:30,600 --> 00:32:35,680
common man around his presidency with when white men,

526
00:32:35,680 --> 00:32:39,240
I should say, were given the right to vote based on age,

527
00:32:39,240 --> 00:32:41,640
as opposed to if you had property

528
00:32:41,640 --> 00:32:45,520
or paid a certain amount in land owning taxes.

529
00:32:45,520 --> 00:32:48,040
So it was the evolution of voting rights

530
00:32:48,040 --> 00:32:50,740
towards white men over the age of 21

531
00:32:50,740 --> 00:32:52,720
as opposed to landowners.

532
00:32:52,720 --> 00:32:54,560
Quick side note, opponents of Jackson

533
00:32:54,560 --> 00:32:59,240
during the 1828 election called him a word that means donkey.

534
00:33:00,200 --> 00:33:02,520
But it was an epithet that Jackson embraced.

535
00:33:02,520 --> 00:33:05,600
He even put images of donkeys on his campaign posters.

536
00:33:05,600 --> 00:33:07,580
And that is when that all started.

537
00:33:07,580 --> 00:33:09,400
And the party that went up against Jackson

538
00:33:09,400 --> 00:33:13,000
was the National Republican Party.

539
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:16,240
But they were just as often known as the anti-Jacksonians.

540
00:33:16,240 --> 00:33:19,920
They did not like what Jackson had done to the role of president.

541
00:33:19,920 --> 00:33:27,600
He took steps to concentrate power and to make sure that he was a very powerful executive.

542
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:36,080
He had taken certain policies that really infringed on the rights of Native Americans and the rights of states.

543
00:33:36,080 --> 00:33:43,440
And this sort of sowed the seeds of what would gradually flare up into the start of the modern

544
00:33:43,440 --> 00:33:49,760
Republican Party and also the disagreements that slared out into the Civil War.

545
00:33:50,560 --> 00:33:59,040
So remember, part of the story is that the parties want to maintain cohesion. They understand that

546
00:33:59,040 --> 00:34:05,520
it's difficult for minor parties, third parties, or smaller parties to win the presidency. It's

547
00:34:05,520 --> 00:34:11,760
difficult for them to win Senate seats or seats in the House of Representatives and be appointed

548
00:34:11,760 --> 00:34:18,160
to Senate seats. And because they are worried about splitting their power, they are trying to do

549
00:34:18,160 --> 00:34:24,400
everything they can to remain together. And one of the things that splits them up more than anything

550
00:34:24,400 --> 00:34:30,640
else, kind of, I would say the thing that stresses the party the most is a conversation about slavery.

551
00:34:30,640 --> 00:34:35,280
And if we want to have a party that is unified in the north and in the south

552
00:34:35,280 --> 00:34:40,360
We can't have this conversation about slavery because people in the north are going to disagree from people in the south

553
00:34:40,640 --> 00:34:45,760
So we end up with these parties that exist in different ways because the one thing that they

554
00:34:46,320 --> 00:34:50,640
Probably should be talking about they are not talking about so we end up with these cleavages

555
00:34:51,200 --> 00:34:55,840
Kind of for that reason where we have a northern Democratic Party that looks different from a southern Democratic Party

556
00:34:55,840 --> 00:35:01,600
But eventually they do have that conversation and we end up with a Republican party that's more dominant in the north

557
00:35:02,080 --> 00:35:05,120
because they have had the conversation to come down on the side of

558
00:35:06,000 --> 00:35:08,000
Black people come down against

559
00:35:08,640 --> 00:35:10,640
Slavery for various reasons again

560
00:35:10,960 --> 00:35:12,960
Not all of them on the up and up

561
00:35:13,320 --> 00:35:17,200
Settle where we have a party again Republican Party in the north a Democratic Party

562
00:35:17,200 --> 00:35:23,400
That's kind of dominant in the south and then we have some kind of debate about who's gonna win the West and what the farmers want

563
00:35:23,400 --> 00:35:29,300
and whether or not the parties will be willing to bend to the demands of the

564
00:35:29,300 --> 00:35:33,180
people who are in the West and who now have the ability to vote in influenced

565
00:35:33,180 --> 00:35:39,220
politics too. All right now I want to learn about that shift. How does the party

566
00:35:39,220 --> 00:35:46,220
that is the party of slavery, the party of the Ku Klux Klan, become the party of

567
00:35:46,220 --> 00:35:51,140
the civil rights movement, the party that gives us our first African-American

568
00:35:51,140 --> 00:35:55,940
president. So if you want to sound really smart with your friends, if you like know a political

569
00:35:55,940 --> 00:36:01,540
scientist and you want to get their gears going, you just say realignment. Because that is the

570
00:36:01,540 --> 00:36:09,060
one word answer to that question. Realignment happens and the parties change. And so the

571
00:36:09,060 --> 00:36:13,300
political scientists argue about how realignment happens. I'm in the camp of people who think

572
00:36:13,300 --> 00:36:19,220
realignment is a slow and gradual process. The short version is that America changes. So in the

573
00:36:19,220 --> 00:36:23,340
And the story that we've been telling up to this point, there are folks who live in the

574
00:36:23,340 --> 00:36:25,640
south, there are folks who live in the north.

575
00:36:25,640 --> 00:36:30,840
We don't yet have like a large wave of immigrants coming into the United States.

576
00:36:30,840 --> 00:36:35,040
So we get an industrial revolution, we get a world war, we get immigrants coming into

577
00:36:35,040 --> 00:36:41,060
the United States, and we don't yet in the nation have rules that are structured to prevent

578
00:36:41,060 --> 00:36:46,440
them from participating in the ways that we try to prevent them from participating now.

579
00:36:46,440 --> 00:36:48,480
So it's kind of easier to get to citizenship,

580
00:36:48,480 --> 00:36:51,160
easier to get to participation in politics.

581
00:36:51,160 --> 00:36:52,880
And so a part of the answer

582
00:36:52,880 --> 00:36:55,160
about how the Democratic Party in particular

583
00:36:55,160 --> 00:36:58,200
becomes the party of the people,

584
00:36:58,200 --> 00:37:00,680
as opposed to the party of the slave owners

585
00:37:00,680 --> 00:37:04,440
or the party of Southern business interests,

586
00:37:04,440 --> 00:37:06,960
has to do with their decisions to

587
00:37:06,960 --> 00:37:09,800
or attempts to win elections,

588
00:37:09,800 --> 00:37:13,200
particularly I would say at the state and local level,

589
00:37:13,200 --> 00:37:17,100
and to speak to the needs of immigrants.

590
00:37:17,100 --> 00:37:19,220
Now I do want to step in here and say that the North

591
00:37:19,220 --> 00:37:21,860
and the South are not just one unified thing.

592
00:37:21,860 --> 00:37:22,860
That's unfair.

593
00:37:22,860 --> 00:37:24,720
There were people who posed slavery in the South,

594
00:37:24,720 --> 00:37:26,800
people who supported it in the North,

595
00:37:26,800 --> 00:37:30,340
whites-only signs, other forms of segregation,

596
00:37:30,340 --> 00:37:32,820
in schools, businesses, housing,

597
00:37:32,820 --> 00:37:35,380
those existed in the North as well as the South.

598
00:37:35,380 --> 00:37:38,380
And as Kenesha told me, African-American voters

599
00:37:38,380 --> 00:37:40,100
are a huge part of the story.

600
00:37:40,100 --> 00:37:43,420
It's not just immigrants who are flooding into the cities.

601
00:37:43,420 --> 00:37:45,260
Black people are flooding into the cities.

602
00:37:45,260 --> 00:37:50,340
The Great Migration brings about six and a half million black people from the South into

603
00:37:50,340 --> 00:37:51,860
the North.

604
00:37:51,860 --> 00:37:57,660
And parties on the ground, local party leaders, mayors, alderman, governors, have to contend

605
00:37:57,660 --> 00:38:04,220
with how they might get this block of voters to support them as well, which makes them

606
00:38:04,220 --> 00:38:09,980
take kind of steps towards civil rights that they might not otherwise take.

607
00:38:09,980 --> 00:38:15,260
And then we have the Great Depression in the 1930s.

608
00:38:15,260 --> 00:38:21,020
President Franklin Delano Roosevelt and his party, the Democrats, said people are suffering.

609
00:38:21,020 --> 00:38:23,140
We need to do something.

610
00:38:23,140 --> 00:38:25,300
And what they did was the new deal.

611
00:38:25,300 --> 00:38:26,940
Relief, reform, recovery.

612
00:38:26,940 --> 00:38:29,940
This is more than a political campaign.

613
00:38:29,940 --> 00:38:33,100
It is a cold wall.

614
00:38:33,100 --> 00:38:37,900
What this did was further cement the notion that the Democratic Party is the party of

615
00:38:37,900 --> 00:38:43,100
big government spending on domestic programs and social welfare programs.

616
00:38:43,100 --> 00:38:48,100
But the civil rights movement, that initially was more allied by geography than by party.

617
00:38:48,100 --> 00:38:53,500
Almost 100% of Northern Democrats in Congress supported the Civil Rights Act of 1964,

618
00:38:53,500 --> 00:38:57,740
but so too did 85% of Northern Republicans.

619
00:38:57,740 --> 00:39:02,100
Just 9% of Southern Dems and zero Southern Republicans supported it in Congress.

620
00:39:02,100 --> 00:39:03,500
So here's Patty Riley.

621
00:39:03,500 --> 00:39:06,400
He's a professor of history and humanities at Reed College.

622
00:39:06,400 --> 00:39:09,960
But I mean, I think the key thing is that the Democratic Party has just,

623
00:39:09,960 --> 00:39:16,360
it's no longer become possible for Southern white supremacists to remain in the party

624
00:39:16,360 --> 00:39:19,720
because the, because the National Party has moved so hard on civil rights.

625
00:39:19,920 --> 00:39:22,960
I mean, that's Johnson's, Lyndon Johnson's famous line.

626
00:39:23,440 --> 00:39:25,080
We lost the South for a generation.

627
00:39:25,080 --> 00:39:27,880
I mean, it turns out to be true, a generation and more at this point.

628
00:39:29,240 --> 00:39:32,480
So I think effectively the South kind of becomes up for grabs

629
00:39:33,240 --> 00:39:35,760
because they're not going to remain in the Democratic Party.

630
00:39:35,760 --> 00:39:37,740
So is someone going to capitalize on them?

631
00:39:37,740 --> 00:39:39,720
And the Republicans do.

632
00:39:39,720 --> 00:39:42,000
I mean, that's just what happens.

633
00:39:42,000 --> 00:39:44,720
I don't want to sound cynical here.

634
00:39:44,720 --> 00:39:47,760
It just kind of sounds like a big part of the reason

635
00:39:47,760 --> 00:39:52,760
that the Democrats completely reversed their positions.

636
00:39:52,760 --> 00:39:58,760
And just about everything was not purely because of ideals,

637
00:39:58,760 --> 00:40:01,200
but to court voters.

638
00:40:01,200 --> 00:40:03,040
Well, I mean, I'm a political scientist,

639
00:40:03,040 --> 00:40:05,600
so I think everything is about political strategy,

640
00:40:05,600 --> 00:40:08,000
political expediency.

641
00:40:08,000 --> 00:40:11,120
But yes, I think that one of the kind of biggest,

642
00:40:11,120 --> 00:40:15,160
broadest ways of understanding party history

643
00:40:15,160 --> 00:40:19,720
is that parties are trying to, one, maintain themselves.

644
00:40:19,720 --> 00:40:22,200
And then parties as groups who are willing to court

645
00:40:22,200 --> 00:40:25,280
coalitions in order to keep or maintain power.

646
00:40:25,280 --> 00:40:26,840
Black people are here.

647
00:40:27,720 --> 00:40:30,620
They wanna have some kind of intervention on civil rights.

648
00:40:30,620 --> 00:40:32,000
We're not opposed to that.

649
00:40:32,000 --> 00:40:34,240
That seems like it could be okay for us.

650
00:40:34,240 --> 00:40:38,040
We think that they would help us win these local and state

651
00:40:38,040 --> 00:40:38,560
elections.

652
00:40:38,560 --> 00:40:40,560
We think that because they live in these states

653
00:40:40,560 --> 00:40:42,680
with large electoral college votes,

654
00:40:42,680 --> 00:40:45,040
they could help us win the presidential election.

655
00:40:45,040 --> 00:40:48,520
Let's test out a coalition between Black people

656
00:40:48,520 --> 00:40:49,760
and the Democratic Party.

657
00:40:49,760 --> 00:40:51,240
So it's the same kind of thing.

658
00:40:51,240 --> 00:40:55,280
Parties kind of moving and shape shifting

659
00:40:55,280 --> 00:40:59,360
as they encounter groups so that they can maintain dominance.

660
00:40:59,360 --> 00:41:09,720
So, thinking about like the party today versus the party then, there's a lot of arguing going

661
00:41:09,720 --> 00:41:14,800
on on social media about the problematic history of both parties, right?

662
00:41:14,800 --> 00:41:21,480
And I'm just wondering, like, given how different the parties are today from how they were at

663
00:41:21,480 --> 00:41:23,800
their genesis, is that even fair to do?

664
00:41:23,800 --> 00:41:28,160
Yeah, people taking the Democratic Party to task for being the party of the KKK.

665
00:41:28,160 --> 00:41:30,240
I asked Patty about that specifically.

666
00:41:30,240 --> 00:41:34,800
That accusation in some sense, it seems like it has power partly because maybe we are just

667
00:41:34,800 --> 00:41:40,320
not open and public enough about just how deep and powerful the history of white supremacy

668
00:41:40,320 --> 00:41:42,480
is in the United States.

669
00:41:42,480 --> 00:41:47,160
It shouldn't be possible for us to continue to romanticize the past.

670
00:41:47,160 --> 00:41:53,520
So those accusations seem to have power just because we need to be more open.

671
00:41:53,520 --> 00:42:00,620
So finally, with all that history under our belt, I asked Kanisha about the party going

672
00:42:00,620 --> 00:42:06,600
forward if she thinks there might be another realignment.

673
00:42:06,600 --> 00:42:09,820
The Democratic Party is a big tent party.

674
00:42:09,820 --> 00:42:11,800
Keep these coalitions in mind.

675
00:42:11,800 --> 00:42:18,880
The Democratic Party has to please immigrants, black people, gay people, progressive white

676
00:42:18,880 --> 00:42:19,880
people.

677
00:42:19,880 --> 00:42:22,600
They just business interest for some people.

678
00:42:22,600 --> 00:42:26,020
Like people, there's just so many groups of people

679
00:42:26,020 --> 00:42:27,960
they have to be worried about.

680
00:42:27,960 --> 00:42:30,280
When you think about the Democratic Party or any party,

681
00:42:30,280 --> 00:42:32,560
particularly in a national election,

682
00:42:32,560 --> 00:42:35,040
they have to get in a room and fight it out.

683
00:42:35,040 --> 00:42:37,160
A party platform's only so long,

684
00:42:37,160 --> 00:42:39,360
and you know, not everybody's gonna read it,

685
00:42:39,360 --> 00:42:41,080
but it matters a lot to the party,

686
00:42:41,080 --> 00:42:43,320
and it matters a lot to the messaging of the party.

687
00:42:43,320 --> 00:42:48,320
And so how do I say I really care about urban development

688
00:42:49,880 --> 00:42:54,880
And I really don't like displacement of people as a result of gentrification.

689
00:42:54,880 --> 00:42:58,880
In some instances that stuff is gonna be in conflict.

690
00:42:58,880 --> 00:43:05,880
And so the Democratic Party has this difficult road to travel because they have to please all these different groups of people,

691
00:43:05,880 --> 00:43:08,880
and these different groups of people have different interests.

692
00:43:08,880 --> 00:43:12,880
So the Democratic Party has come a long way.

693
00:43:12,880 --> 00:43:17,880
Changing names, switching positions on the way to the blue party we think of today.

694
00:43:17,880 --> 00:43:21,960
And that's the thing, these parties are always changing.

695
00:43:21,960 --> 00:43:25,080
So it's really hard to say what a Democrat is,

696
00:43:25,080 --> 00:43:26,240
because there's not one answer

697
00:43:26,240 --> 00:43:28,240
and it depends on a ton of other things.

698
00:43:32,400 --> 00:43:34,120
Well, we did it Hannah.

